# Valve's arm64 steamcmd, for the OpenGamepadUI Steam plugin. A CHANNEL pin: it names where Valve
# publishes steamcmd, not one release of it.
#
# Read by /usr/lib/retroforge/steamcmd-fetch, which ~/.steam/steamcmd/steamcmd.sh runs while that is
# still RetroForge's stub (/usr/lib/retroforge/steamcmd-stub, which /usr/lib/retroforge/install-steamcmd
# puts there before the shell starts). The first time the plugin runs steamcmd, the stub has the
# helper find, download and check what Valve publishes today, renames it over itself and runs it.
# From then on ~/.steam/steamcmd is Valve's own steamcmd, which updates itself from the same manifest.
#
# WHY. The plugin (ShadowBlip/OpenGamepadUI-steam, core/steam_client.gd) runs
# ~/.steam/steamcmd/steamcmd.sh for the library, installs and updates. When that file is missing it
# downloads Valve's x86 bootstrapper (steamcmd_linux.tar.gz: an i386 binary), which cannot run here.
# It downloads only when steamcmd.sh is missing, so the stub sitting there stops it.
#
# Not in the image: everything that can be downloaded is.
#
# WHAT IS RESOLVED, and how each piece is checked:
#   cdn + manifest   Valve's update manifest, <cdn>/<manifest>, read fresh at every first run
#                    (client-update.steamstatic.com serves the same bytes). Its package files are
#                    content-addressed and replaced on every release, which is why no file is pinned.
#   package          the manifest's IsBootstrapperPackage. Its "file" is fetched from <cdn>/ and
#                    refused unless its sha256 is the manifest's "sha2" for it. On 2026-10-16 (manifest
#                    version 1791918946) it was steamcmd_linuxarm64.zip.36b2d2cc..., 3.7 MB, holding
#                    linuxarm64/steamcmd alone (mode 0666, no execute bit). That binary downloads the
#                    rest of steamcmd (steamcmd_bins_linuxarm64: steamclient.so, crashhandler.so, ...)
#                    on its first start, then exits 42 for its launcher to start it again.
#   installer        Valve's installer tarball, used for ONE file: steamcmd.sh, the launcher. No
#                    package of the manifest carries it (arm64 or x86), and steamcmd never writes it.
#   script_sha256    that steamcmd.sh's own sha256, not the tarball's. The tarball sits at an
#                    unversioned URL that Valve replaces in place, but its binaries are never used
#                    (they lag the manifest: the tarball still had the 2026-07-27 build on
#                    2026-10-16), so a republished tarball changes nothing here unless the 2.5 kB
#                    launcher inside it changes. The x86 tarball's launcher has not changed since 2018.
#                    If the bootstrapper zip ever carries a steamcmd.sh, the manifest vouches for that
#                    one and the tarball is not downloaded.
# Nothing is used unchecked: the zip against the manifest (read over HTTPS from Valve's update host;
# redirects may only go to https), steamcmd.sh against script_sha256. Then the shape:
# linuxarm64/steamcmd must be an arm64 ELF, steamcmd.sh must pick linuxarm64 and not be the stub.
#
# Valve's steamcmd.sh picks linuxarm64 on `uname -m` = aarch64 and linux32 otherwise. The binaries
# need only glibc (GLIBC_2.30, no Steam Runtime).
#
# ARMv8.0 (SM6115): NOT LSE-clean. steamcmd has ~300 unguarded LSE atomics (ldaddal, casal, swpa,
# ...), crashhandler.so ~135, steamerrorreporter 5, with no outline-atomic helpers, which is the same
# toolchain as Valve's arm64 client (docs/steam-arm64.md). The SM8250/SM8550/SM8650 boards run it
# natively. The Air X needs kernel/patches/0025 (LSE emulation) or it SIGILLs as soon as steamcmd goes
# multithreaded. It is Valve's binary, so this cannot be fixed with a rebuild.
#
# WHEN IT FAILS. Offline, or a sha256 that does not match the manifest (a broken download): the stub
# exits 1 and stays, and the next start tries again. "Valve's steamcmd.sh has changed": Valve edited
# the launcher. Read the new one (it must still pick linuxarm64 and restart steamcmd on exit 42), then
# set script_sha256 to its sha256. Until that ships, a player who has not run steamcmd yet gets that
# error and no steamcmd. Players who have are not affected: their copy is Valve's, it updates itself,
# and install-steamcmd never replaces a working arm64 copy.
#
# REPRODUCIBLE INSTALL (optional, off): uncomment fixed_file + fixed_sha256 together to skip the
# manifest and install that one package file from <cdn>/ (steamcmd.sh still comes as above). Nothing
# says how long Valve keeps an old package file on its CDN, so leave these off in a release.
name: steamcmd
cdn: https://media.steampowered.com/client
manifest: steam_cmd_linuxarm64
package: steamcmd_linuxarm64
installer: https://steamcdn-a.akamaihd.net/client/installer/steamcmd_linuxarm64.tar.gz
script_sha256: 866a74c3e1d934d3d08c061be521855fdeb799311d586f5d2e049b9a378f794b
#fixed_file: steamcmd_linuxarm64.zip.36b2d2cc5cad9b9d82bc7339dd6554b39daa1d3c
#fixed_sha256: 08ef42ef2fba30ced5fed45fc185e1eb9613075e4dc7a9f17368d2900d5f36f0
